package com.example.paoabacus.data

val PAO_DATA: List<PAOEntry> = listOf(
    PAOEntry("00", "Ozzy Osbourne", "Chomping", "a bat", "Real Person", "Heavy Metal Musician",
        "John Michael 'Ozzy' Osbourne is an English heavy metal singer who rose to fame as the lead vocalist of Black Sabbath. Nicknamed the 'Prince of Darkness,' he is famous for his wild stage persona — including the infamous 1982 concert where he bit the head off a live bat.",
        "Real life (1982 Des Moines concert)",
        "Ozzy Osbourne, the heavy metal singer, is on stage with spotlights blazing. Look at his name: O-z-z-y O-s-b-o-u-r-n-e. The two capital O's are two wide-open eyes. Those two O-eyes become his staring eyes as he chomps a bat between them on stage. Smell the copper blood and hot stage lights. Taste the iron on his tongue. Hear him scream 'I'M STILL HERE!' and the crowd roar, the microphone feedback whining. Feel the wet bat fur sticking to his lips. He spits it out and grins, the two O-eyes still wide open, forever."),
    PAOEntry("01", "Neo", "Dodging", "a bullet", "Fictional", "Sci-Fi Action Hero (The Matrix)",
        "Neo is the protagonist of The Matrix trilogy, played by Keanu Reeves. A computer hacker who discovers reality is a simulation, he learns to bend the rules of the Matrix and becomes 'The One' destined to free humanity.",
        "The Matrix (1999), Warner Bros.",
        "Neo, the hacker, watches code rearranging on his screen — N-E-O flickering into O-N-E. The name rearranges: ONE. He dodges a bullet in slow motion, his body bending back, one arm reaching forward. The round bullet passes through the air where he stood. Feel the wind of the bullet on his cheek. Taste the metallic tang of fear. The bullet punches a round hole through the wall behind him. He whispers 'I am the ONE' as the code freezes."),
    PAOEntry("02", "James Bond", "Counting", "a watch", "Fictional", "Spy / MI6 Agent (007)",
        "James Bond, codename 007, is a fictional British Secret Service agent created by writer Ian Fleming in 1953. Known for his suave demeanor, martinis, and high-tech gadgets, he has been portrayed by seven actors across 25+ films.",
        "Dr. No (1962) — Eon Productions",
        "James Bond, the spy in a tuxedo, sits at a casino bar sipping his martini. His watch beeps a countdown: 'TO... TO... TO...' Each TO sounds like the number 2. Taste the gin and vermouth on his tongue. Smell the sharp cologne and cold casino air. Feel the cold gold of the watch against his wrist. Hear the beeps quicken as the seconds tick. A red laser dot appears on his chest. On the final TO, the watch explodes — smoke, glass, chaos. Bond doesn't flinch."),
    PAOEntry("03", "Harry Potter", "Summoning", "a broomstick", "Fictional", "Wizard (Harry Potter series)",
        "Harry Potter is the protagonist of J.K. Rowling's fantasy series, a young wizard who survives the killing curse as a baby and grows up to defeat the dark wizard Lord Voldemort. He is famous for his lightning-bolt scar and his skill at Quidditch.",
        "Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone (1997), J.K. Rowling",
        "Harry Potter, the young wizard, flicks his wand — and it curls like a 3, coiling around a rolling tank wheel that's crashing through the castle hallway. Smell the spellfire and scorched stone. Hear the wheel grinding and Harry screaming 'ACCIO!' Taste the magic on his tongue like copper and mint. Feel the wand's heat in his palm. The wand yanks the wheel, the broomstick flies out, and Harry rides off as the wheel explodes into golden sparks."),
    PAOEntry("04", "Thor", "Calling", "Mjolnir hammer", "Fictional", "Norse God & Marvel Superhero",
        "Thor is the Asgardian God of Thunder from Norse mythology, adapted into Marvel Comics in 1962 and the Marvel Cinematic Universe in 2011. Played by Chris Hemsworth, he wields the enchanted hammer Mjolnir — forged by dwarves — which returns to his hand when thrown.",
        "Norse Mythology / Thor (2011), Marvel Cinematic Universe",
        "Thor, the God of Thunder, raises his hand to the storm. His hammer Mjolnir — head shaped like a triangular 4 — spins through the rain toward him. CRACK! Lightning strikes the ground in a round scorched 0 as the hammer lands in his hand. Smell the ozone and wet earth. Taste the metallic charge on his tongue. Feel the electricity crawling up his arm. He screams 'MJOLNIR!' and 4 bolts strike in a circle around him. Loki flinches in the shadows."),
    PAOEntry("05", "Sherlock Holmes", "Magnifying", "a pipe", "Fictional", "Detective (Victorian London)",
        "Sherlock Holmes is a consulting detective created by Sir Arthur Conan Doyle in 1887. Famous for his brilliant deductive reasoning, his pipe, deerstalker cap, and his partnership with Dr. John Watson, he has appeared in over 60 original stories and countless adaptations.",
        "A Study in Scarlet (1887), Arthur Conan Doyle",
        "Sherlock Holmes, the detective, leans on his walking cane as he peers through a spyglass at a black hole opening in his study floor. His cane slips, the spyglass drops toward the hole, and he mutters 'Elementary.' Smell the damp London fog and old tobacco. Taste the bitter tea on his tongue. Feel the cold brass of the spyglass. Hear the black hole hum like a broken organ. He catches the spyglass just before it falls in, and the hole closes. 'Fascinating,' he whispers."),
    PAOEntry("06", "Darth Vader", "Igniting", "a lightsaber", "Fictional", "Sith Lord (Star Wars)",
        "Darth Vader, born Anakin Skywalker, is the central antagonist of the original Star Wars trilogy. A former Jedi who turned to the Dark Side, he is encased in black armor and serves the Galactic Empire, wielding a red lightsaber. He is redeemed by his son Luke in Return of the Jedi.",
        "Star Wars: Episode IV — A New Hope (1977)",
        "Darth Vader, the Sith Lord, ignites his lightsaber — but it's inside a crystal ball, its hilt curled like a balloon tail. The crystal shatters into a hundred glowing shards. Smell the scorched metal and burnt ozone. Taste the bitter ash on his breath. Hear his mechanical breathing and the hum of the blade. Feel the heat radiating from the beam. He breathes 'I find your lack of crystal disturbing' as the shards rain down."),
    PAOEntry("07", "Wonder Woman", "Lassoing", "a tank", "Fictional", "DC Superhero (Amazonian Princess)",
        "Wonder Woman is the Amazonian princess Diana of Themyscira, created by William Moulton Marston in 1941. She possesses superhuman strength, flight, and wields the Lasso of Truth, bulletproof bracelets, and a tiara. Played by Gal Gadot in the DC Extended Universe.",
        "All Star Comics #8 (1941) / Wonder Woman (2017), DC",
        "Wonder Woman, the Amazon warrior, spins her lasso — it curls like a boomerang, arcing toward a giant blood orange. She yanks the orange over her head and it bursts into juice. Smell the citrus and sweat. Taste the sweet tang of orange on her lips. Feel the lasso's warm leather in her palms. Hear the orange squelch and the tank explode behind her. She screams 'I NEED A DRINK!' as juice rains down."),
    PAOEntry("08", "Mario", "Stomping", "a Goomba", "Fictional", "Video Game Character (Nintendo)",
        "Mario is the flagship mascot of Nintendo, created by Shigeru Miyamoto in 1981. An Italian plumber from the Mushroom Kingdom, he is famous for jumping on enemies like Goombas, eating mushrooms to grow, and rescuing Princess Peach from Bowser.",
        "Donkey Kong (1981) / Super Mario Bros. (1985), Nintendo",
        "Mario, the plumber, stomps the top of a Goomba — a creature shaped like an 8, two stacked mushrooms. POP! The top mushroom squashes flat, and a round coin pops out of the bottom. Smell the earthy mushroom stink. Taste a mushroom he ate earlier — sweet and chewy. Feel the coin's weight in his palm. Hear the coin chime and the Goomba squeak. He yells 'Wahoo!' as more coins rain down."),
    PAOEntry("09", "Batman", "Grappling", "a gargoyle", "Fictional", "DC Superhero (Gotham Vigilante)",
        "Batman is the alter ego of billionaire Bruce Wayne, created by Bob Kane and Bill Finger in 1939. After witnessing his parents' murder, he trains to become Gotham City's Dark Knight, using his intellect, martial arts, and gadgets like the grappling gun to fight crime.",
        "Detective Comics #27 (1939), DC Comics",
        "Batman, the Dark Knight, fires his grapple at a hooked staff leaning on a giant pomegranate in an alley. The staff catches his line, and he swings — the pomegranate bursts into red seeds. Smell the wet Gotham pavement and blood-red juice. Taste the metallic grime in the air. Feel the grapple's cold steel in his gloved hand. Hear the seeds ping on the fire escape. He whispers 'I'm vengeance' as the seeds rain down."),
    PAOEntry("10", "Albert Einstein", "Theorizing", "a blackhole", "Real Person", "Theoretical Physicist",
        "Albert Einstein (1879–1955) was a German-born theoretical physicist widely considered the father of modern physics. He developed the theory of relativity and the mass–energy equivalence formula E = mc², and won the 1921 Nobel Prize in Physics.",
        "Real life / Nobel Prize 1921",
        "Albert Einstein, the physicist, stands straight as a lighthouse beam, writing equations in the air. His wild hair curls into a round tambourine shape around his head. He writes 'E=mc²' as the tambourine shatters — ringing a single note. Smell the chalk dust and old books. Taste the woody pencil on his lips. Feel the beam's heat on his face. Hear the tambourine ring one last time as the equation vanishes in light."),
    PAOEntry("11", "Eeyore", "Plodding", "a rusty nail", "Fictional", "Children's Book Character (Winnie the Pooh)",
        "Eeyore is a gloomy, pessimistic donkey from A.A. Milne's Winnie the Pooh stories. Known for his droopy tail (which is often lost), his melancholy demeanor, and his habit of expecting the worst, he is one of the Hundred Acre Wood's most iconic residents.",
        "Winnie-the-Pooh (1926), A.A. Milne",
        "Eeyore, the gloomy donkey, plods forward on two stiff legs. He steps on a rusty nail — CLANK. He sighs 'Oh bother' as the nail sticks to his hoof. Smell the wet grass and old rust. Taste the bitterness of a thistle he ate earlier. Feel the nail dragging with each step. Hear the nail screech on stone as he walks on, dragging it behind him like a ball and chain."),
    PAOEntry("12", "Santa Claus", "Sliding", "a reindeer", "Fictional", "Christmas Folklore Figure",
        "Santa Claus is a legendary figure from Western Christmas folklore, based on Saint Nicholas. He is said to deliver gifts to children worldwide on Christmas Eve, flying in a sleigh pulled by reindeer like Rudolph, and sliding down chimneys.",
        "Western folklore / 'A Visit from St. Nicholas' (1823)",
        "Santa Claus, the jolly old man, slides down a candy cane — landing on a snake slithering across his sleigh. HISS! The snake coils, the candy cane snaps, and Santa yells 'HO HO HO!' Smell the peppermint and reindeer fur. Taste the sugar on his lips. Feel the cold snow and slick candy in his gloves. Hear the sleigh bells and the snake's rattle. He shakes his fist and the snake slithers into the snow."),
    PAOEntry("13", "Indiana Jones", "Whipping", "a snake", "Fictional", "Archaeologist & Adventurer",
        "Dr. Henry 'Indiana' Jones Jr. is a fictional archaeology professor and adventurer created by George Lucas, played by Harrison Ford. Famous for his fedora, leather jacket, and bullwhip, he has a notorious fear of snakes and hunts ancient relics across the globe.",
        "Raiders of the Lost Ark (1981), Lucasfilm",
        "Indiana Jones, the archaeologist, whips his leather whip — but the handle locks to a giant cobra head that rears up at him. Smell the jungle damp and snake musk. Taste the dust of the temple on his lips. Feel the whip's worn leather in his palm. Hear the cobra hiss and his own scream: 'SNAKES! WHY'D IT HAVE TO BE SNAKES!' The cobra swallows his fedora as he dives behind a stone pillar."),
    PAOEntry("14", "Beyoncé", "Singing", "a glitter mic", "Real Person", "Pop & R&B Singer",
        "Beyoncé Giselle Knowles-Carter is an American singer, songwriter, and actress. Rising to fame with Destiny's Child, she became a global solo superstar with 30+ Grammy Awards — the most of any artist — known for her powerhouse vocals and electrifying performances.",
        "Real life / Destiny's Child (1997) → Solo career (2003)",
        "Beyoncé, the pop superstar, sings into her glitter mic while standing on a tall marble statue — with a picnic table balanced on her head. Smell the stage smoke and perfume. Taste the gloss on her lips. Feel the mic's sparkle against her palm. Hear her high note shatter the picnic table into splinters. The statue wobbles beneath her. She yells 'I AM BEYONCÉ!' and lands perfectly on her feet, still singing."),
    PAOEntry("15", "Homelander", "Lasering", "a plane", "Fictional", "Superhero Antagonist (The Boys)",
        "Homelander is the primary antagonist of the Amazon Prime series The Boys, played by Antony Starr. The most powerful superhero on Earth, he is a narcissistic, murderous leader of The Seven who hides his cruelty behind a patriotic public image.",
        "The Boys (2019), Amazon Prime Video",
        "Homelander, the narcissistic superhero, fires his eye-beams through a sharp spike and a spyglass. The beams split into a hundred rays, and a plane explodes in the sky. Smell the burnt fuel and metal. Taste the power on his tongue like electricity. Feel the heat of his own beams on his cheeks. Hear the plane's explosion and the screams of the crew. He smiles: 'I can do anything.' The spyglass shatters, the spike falls."),
    PAOEntry("16", "Shrek", "Roaring", "a swamp", "Fictional", "Animated Ogre (DreamWorks)",
        "Shrek is the protagonist of DreamWorks' animated franchise, voiced by Mike Myers. A grumpy but kind-hearted ogre who lives alone in a swamp, he embarks on adventures that teach him about friendship, love, and self-acceptance.",
        "Shrek (2001), DreamWorks Animation",
        "Shrek, the grumpy ogre, roars in his swamp — but the swamp sits inside a giant snail shell, and a fountain pen is writing his name on the shell. He roars 'GET OUT OF MY SWAMP!' and the pen snaps, ink splattering everywhere. Smell the swamp mud and stale ink. Taste the moss on his tongue. Feel the cold shell against his back. Hear the shell crack as the swamp spills out. He lands in the mud and says 'Better.'"),
    PAOEntry("17", "Michael Jordan", "Dunking", "a hoop", "Real Person", "NBA Basketball Legend",
        "Michael Jeffrey Jordan (born 1963) is a former American professional basketball player widely regarded as the greatest of all time. He won six NBA championships with the Chicago Bulls and five MVP awards. His 'Air Jordan' sneakers became a cultural phenomenon.",
        "Real life / Chicago Bulls (1984–1998, 2001–2003)",
        "Michael Jordan, the basketball legend, catches a lacrosse ball mid-air and dunks it through a hoop mounted on a ship mast. The mast snaps. Smell the gym sweat and rubber floor. Taste the salt of his own sweat. Feel the ball's pebbled leather in his palm. Hear the crowd roar and the rim rattle. He yells 'I CAN FLY!' as he lands on his feet, arms spread wide."),
    PAOEntry("18", "Spider-Man", "Thwipping", "a web", "Fictional", "Marvel Superhero",
        "Spider-Man is the alter ego of teenager Peter Parker, created by Stan Lee and Steve Ditko in 1962. Bitten by a radioactive spider, he gains super strength, wall-crawling abilities, and a 'spider-sense.' His motto — 'With great power comes great responsibility' — was taught to him by his Uncle Ben.",
        "Amazing Fantasy #15 (1962), Marvel Comics",
        "Spider-Man, the neighborhood hero, shoots a web from his wrist — aimed at two wide-open eyes floating in the sky, while a fountain pen draws the web as it flies. He yells 'THWIP!' The eyes blink. Smell the city smog and spider silk. Taste the adrenaline in his mouth. Feel the web's tension in his wrist. Hear the pen snap and the eyes close. He lands on a rooftop and whispers 'That was weird.'"),
    PAOEntry("19", "The Hulk", "Shattering", "a shoestring", "Fictional", "Marvel Superhero",
        "The Hulk is the alter ego of scientist Dr. Bruce Banner, created by Stan Lee and Jack Kirby in 1962. After being exposed to gamma radiation, Banner transforms into a massive green rage monster whenever he gets angry. His power grows with his anger.",
        "The Incredible Hulk #1 (1962), Marvel Comics",
        "The Hulk, the green rage monster, grabs a toothpick wrapped around a cluster of pomegranates. He snaps the toothpick and the pomegranates explode into red seeds. Smell the crushed fruit and his own sweat. Taste the iron of his bitten lip. Feel the sticky juice on his fingers. Hear him roar 'HULK SMASH!' as seeds rain down. He picks one seed up and eats it."),
    PAOEntry("20", "The Dude", "Guzzling", "a white Russian", "Fictional", "Movie Slacker (The Big Lebowski)",
        "Jeffrey 'The Dude' Lebowski is the protagonist of the Coen Brothers' 1998 cult classic The Big Lebowski, played by Jeff Bridges. A laid-back Los Angeles slacker, he spends his days bowling, drinking White Russians, and getting dragged into bizarre criminal plots.",
        "The Big Lebowski (1998), Coen Brothers",
        "The Dude, the laid-back slacker, tilts his curved arm to lift a bowling ball filled with a White Russian. He drinks, burps, and says 'The Dude abides.' Smell the cream and vodka. Taste the sweetness on his tongue. Feel the cool bowling ball in his palm. Hear the pins crash behind him as the ball rolls down the lane. He says 'That was a strike, man.'"),
    PAOEntry("21", "Captain America", "Hurling", "a shield", "Fictional", "Marvel Superhero",
        "Captain America is the alter ego of Steve Rogers, created by Joe Simon and Jack Kirby in 1941. A frail young man who volunteered for a super-soldier experiment during WWII, he became a symbol of American heroism. His iconic vibranium shield can be thrown like a boomerang.",
        "Captain America Comics #1 (1941), Marvel Comics",
        "Captain America, the super soldier, hurls his shield — a baseball bat bent into a hook. It curves through the air, smacks a Hydra agent, and returns to him. Smell the sweat and gunpowder. Taste the copper of his own blood. Feel the leather strap on his arm. Hear the bat whistle and the agent yell as he falls. He yells 'I CAN DO THIS ALL DAY!' and throws it again."),
    PAOEntry("22", "Yoda", "Meditating", "a Jedi Council chamber", "Fictional", "Jedi Master (Star Wars)",
        "Yoda is a legendary Jedi Master in the Star Wars saga, appearing in all three original and prequel trilogies. A small, green, pointy-eared alien who speaks in reversed grammar, he is nearly 900 years old and one of the most powerful Force users ever.",
        "Star Wars: Episode V — The Empire Strikes Back (1980)",
        "Yoda, the Jedi Master, meditates in the Jedi Council chamber. His silhouette splits into two curved swan necks — his head and his back both curving like 2's. The Force lifts his chair as he speaks: 'Do or do not, there is no try.' Smell the swamp muck and damp robes. Taste the bitter Dagobah root on his tongue. Feel the Force humming around him. Hear the room vibrate. His two 2-shapes spin around each other into 22."),
    PAOEntry("23", "Michael Jackson", "Moonwalking", "a glove", "Real Person", "Pop Music Legend",
        "Michael Joseph Jackson (1958–2009) was an American singer, songwriter, and dancer known as the 'King of Pop.' He revolutionized music videos with Thriller and Bad, and popularized the moonwalk — a dance move where he appeared to slide backward while walking forward.",
        "Real life / Motown 25 performance (1983)",
        "Michael Jackson, the King of Pop, moonwalks on a wobbly stool balanced on a leaping dolphin. He spins, the dolphin flips, and the stool wobbles. He yells 'HEE HEE!' Smell the sea salt and stage smoke. Taste the glitter gloss on his lips. Feel the beat under his feet. Hear the crowd scream and the dolphin splash. He lands on the beach, does a spin, and moonwalks away."),
    PAOEntry("24", "Ripley", "Bowling", "a grenade", "Fictional", "Sci-Fi Survivor (Alien franchise)",
        "Ellen Ripley is the protagonist of the Alien film franchise, played by Sigourney Weaver. A warrant officer aboard the Nostromo, she becomes humanity's greatest weapon against the Xenomorphs. Her most iconic moment is when she climbs into a power-loader mech suit to fight the Alien Queen.",
        "Alien (1979), 20th Century Fox",
        "Ripley, the survivor, rolls a grenade across a crescent moon — bowling it toward a triangle sail in the crater. The grenade explodes, the sail tears, and she yells 'GET AWAY FROM HER YOU BITCH!' Smell the sulfur and ash. Taste the blood on her lip. Feel the heat of the blast on her face. Hear the Alien Queen screech. She reloads her flamethrower as the moon cracks."),
    PAOEntry("25", "Hermione Granger", "Studying", "a spellbook", "Fictional", "Witch (Harry Potter series)",
        "Hermione Jean Granger is one of the three central protagonists of the Harry Potter series, played by Emma Watson. A brilliant Muggle-born witch, she is known for her encyclopedic knowledge, her bushy hair, and her loyalty to Harry and Ron.",
        "Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone (1997), J.K. Rowling",
        "Hermione Granger, the brightest witch of her age, hunches over a spellbook, her spine curving like a banana. A hanging key swings above her head. She flips a page and the key clinks against the shelf. Smell the dusty parchment and old ink. Taste the tip of her quill on her lip. Feel the cold iron of the key. Hear her whisper 'It's LeviOHsa, not LevioSAH!' as the book slams shut and the key spins."),
    PAOEntry("26", "The Rock", "Flexing", "a weight", "Real Person", "Wrestler & Actor",
        "Dwayne 'The Rock' Johnson (born 1972) is an American actor and former professional wrestler. One of the highest-paid actors in the world, he is known for his massive physique, his signature 'People's Eyebrow' raise, and his catchphrase 'Can you smell what The Rock is cooking?'",
        "Real life / WWE (1996–2004) → Hollywood",
        "The Rock, the wrestler turned actor, flexes his curved bicep as he lifts a round weight. The weight turns red, and he grunts 'Can you smell what The Rock is cooking?' Smell his cologne and sweat. Taste the energy drink on his tongue. Feel the chalk on his palms. Hear the metal clang as the weight hits the ground. He winks at the camera, and the floor cracks beneath him."),
    PAOEntry("27", "Jack Sparrow", "Swashbuckling", "a compass", "Fictional", "Pirate Captain (Pirates of the Caribbean)",
        "Captain Jack Sparrow is the protagonist of the Pirates of the Caribbean film series, played by Johnny Depp. A witty, eccentric, and perpetually lucky pirate, he wields a compass that doesn't point north but instead points to whatever the holder wants most.",
        "Pirates of the Caribbean: The Curse of the Black Pearl (2003), Disney",
        "Jack Sparrow, the pirate captain, sways on a bent knee as he swings a cutlass. He points his compass, the cutlass slices the air, and he falls into the sea. Smell the salt and rum. Taste the brine on his lips. Feel the compass's cold brass in his palm. Hear the waves crash and his crew yell. He screams 'THIS IS THE DAY YOU ALMOST CAUGHT CAPTAIN JACK SPARROW!' as he sinks."),
    PAOEntry("28", "Pikachu", "Electrocutting", "a thunderbolt", "Fictional", "Pokémon (Electric Mouse)",
        "Pikachu is the most iconic Pokémon, the mascot of the entire franchise. An Electric-type mouse Pokémon with yellow fur, red cheeks, and a lightning-bolt tail, it is the partner of Ash Ketchum in the anime.",
        "Pokémon Red and Green (1996), Nintendo / The Pokémon Company",
        "Pikachu, the electric mouse Pokémon, folds its ear like a wing and fires lightning at a stack of coins. The coins melt into a puddle of gold. Smell the singed air. Taste the static on its tongue. Feel the electricity crackling in its cheeks. Hear the coins sizzle as they melt. Pikachu yells 'Pika-CHU!' and the gold puddle reflects its yellow face."),
    PAOEntry("29", "Deadpool", "Twerking", "a katana", "Fictional", "Marvel Anti-Hero (Merc with a Mouth)",
        "Deadpool is the alter ego of Wade Wilson, created by Rob Liefeld and Fabian Nicieza in 1991. A mercenary with a healing factor that makes him nearly immortal, he is famous for breaking the fourth wall, his crude humor, and his love of chimichangas.",
        "New Mutants #98 (1991), Marvel Comics",
        "Deadpool, the merc with a mouth, twerks in a seashell spiral shape while holding a comet-headed katana. He slices an enemy, then eats a chimichanga. Smell the gunpowder and Mexican food. Taste the spicy salsa on his tongue. Feel the katana's cold hilt in his hand. Hear him yell 'MAXIMUM EFFORT!' as his enemy falls. He winks at the camera and takes another bite."),
    PAOEntry("30", "Gandalf", "Smiting", "a staff", "Fictional", "Wizard (The Lord of the Rings)",
        "Gandalf the Grey (later the White) is a central character in J.R.R. Tolkien's The Lord of the Rings. A Maiar spirit sent to Middle-earth as a wizard, he wields a wooden staff and sword Glamdring. He famously faces the Balrog on the Bridge of Khazad-dûm.",
        "The Fellowship of the Ring (1954), J.R.R. Tolkien",
        "Gandalf, the wizard, smites his staff — two belly rolls of magic ripple across the full moon as the ground cracks. Smell the burning magic and cold night air. Taste the ash on his tongue. Feel the staff's warmth in his hand. Hear him roar 'YOU SHALL NOT PASS!' as the Balrog rises from the flames. The moon cracks in two as the bridge collapses."),
    PAOEntry("31", "Freddie Mercury", "Belting", "a microphone stand", "Real Person", "Rock Music Legend (Queen)",
        "Freddie Mercury (1946–1991) was the lead vocalist of the rock band Queen. Born Farrokh Bulsara in Zanzibar, he was known for his four-octave vocal range, his flamboyant stage presence, and his iconic half-microphone stand.",
        "Real life / Queen (1970–1991)",
        "Freddie Mercury, the Queen frontman, grips a pool cue like a mic stand and belts into it as the crowd roars. His camel hump silhouette rises behind him. Smell the sweat and stage smoke. Taste the gloss on his lips. Feel the cue's smooth wood. Hear the crowd stomp to 'We Will Rock You.' He struts, the cue bends, and the crowd screams."),
    PAOEntry("32", "Luke Skywalker", "Piloting", "an X-wing", "Fictional", "Jedi Knight (Star Wars)",
        "Luke Skywalker is the central hero of the original Star Wars trilogy, played by Mark Hamill. A farm boy from Tatooine who discovers he is the son of Darth Vader, he trains as a Jedi under Obi-Wan Kenobi and Yoda.",
        "Star Wars: Episode IV — A New Hope (1977)",
        "Luke Skywalker, the Jedi, pilots his X-wing — its cockpit curving like an ear and the wings splitting like scorpion tails. He flies into the Death Star trench. Smell the burning engine and space dust. Taste the sweat on his lip. Feel the controls under his gloved hands. Hear 'Use the Force, Luke!' in his headset. He fires the torpedoes, yells 'YAHOO!', and the Death Star explodes."),
    PAOEntry("33", "Voldemort", "Snapping", "a wand", "Fictional", "Dark Wizard (Harry Potter series)",
        "Lord Voldemort, born Tom Marvolo Riddle, is the primary antagonist of the Harry Potter series. One of the most powerful dark wizards of all time, he split his soul into seven Horcruxes to achieve immortality.",
        "Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone (1997), J.K. Rowling",
        "Voldemort, the dark wizard, screams 'AVADA KEDAVRA!' as his two snake-like forms — head splitting into two cobra shapes — snap his wand in half. Smell the dark magic, like burnt hair. Taste the ash on his tongue. Feel the wand splinter in his hand. Hear the spell crack and sparks fly. He screams 'MY WAND!' as Harry laughs in the distance."),
    PAOEntry("34", "The Joker", "Juggling", "a playing card", "Fictional", "DC Supervillain (Batman)",
        "The Joker is Batman's greatest enemy, created by Bob Kane, Bill Finger, and Jerry Robinson in 1940. A chaotic, nihilistic criminal mastermind with green hair and a permanent grin, he uses playing cards as his calling card.",
        "Batman #1 (1940), DC Comics",
        "The Joker, the Clown Prince of Crime, grins like a caterpillar as he climbs a ladder juggling playing cards. He throws one at Batman, cackling 'WHY SO SERIOUS?' Smell the greasepaint and gunpowder. Taste the sour candy on his tongue. Feel the cards slip through his fingers. Hear the card slice Batman's cheek. The Joker runs away, cackling into the night."),
    PAOEntry("35", "Katniss Everdeen", "Aiming", "a bow", "Fictional", "Rebel Hero (The Hunger Games)",
        "Katniss Everdeen is the protagonist of The Hunger Games trilogy, played by Jennifer Lawrence. A skilled archer from District 12, she volunteers as tribute to save her sister Primrose, and becomes the symbol of a revolution against the Capitol.",
        "The Hunger Games (2008), Suzanne Collins",
        "Katniss Everdeen, the archer of District 12, curves her arm like two belly rolls as she draws a fishhook-shaped bow. She releases, and the arrow hits a Peacekeeper. Smell the pine forest and blood. Taste the fear in her mouth. Feel the bowstring burn her fingertips. Hear the arrow zip through the air. She yells 'IF I'M GOING TO DIE, I'M GOING TO DIE AS MYSELF!' and fires again."),
    PAOEntry("36", "Wolverine", "Mangling", "a metal", "Fictional", "Marvel Superhero (X-Men)",
        "Wolverine is the alter ego of James 'Logan' Howlett, created by Roy Thomas, Len Wein, and John Romita Sr. in 1974. A mutant with retractable adamantium claws, a healing factor, and an indestructible skeleton.",
        "The Incredible Hulk #180 (1974), Marvel Comics",
        "Wolverine, the mutant, splits his mustache-shaped face into a snarl as his claws pop out — curved like cow horns. He slashes through a metal door. Smell the scorched metal and his own cigar. Taste the copper of his own blood. Feel the adamantium sliding out of his knuckles. Hear him yell 'I'M THE BEST THERE IS AT WHAT I DO!' as the door falls."),
    PAOEntry("37", "Iron Man", "Blasting", "a repulsor beam", "Fictional", "Marvel Superhero (Avengers)",
        "Iron Man is the alter ego of billionaire inventor Tony Stark, created by Stan Lee, Larry Lieber, Don Heck, and Jack Kirby in 1963. After being captured by terrorists, he builds a powered suit of armor to escape — and keeps upgrading it.",
        "Tales of Suspense #39 (1963), Marvel Comics",
        "Iron Man, the armored Avenger, curls his tongue in concentration as he fires a repulsor beam shaped like a raised spear from his palm. It strikes a Chitauri soldier. Smell the burning circuitry. Taste the metallic tang in his suit's air. Feel the hum of the reactor in his chest. Hear him yell 'I AM IRON MAN!' as he flies off and the beam explodes."),
    PAOEntry("38", "Gollum", "Sneaking", "the One Ring", "Fictional", "Hobbit Corrupted by the Ring (LOTR)",
        "Gollum, originally a Stoorish hobbit named Sméagol, is a tragic character in The Lord of the Rings. Corrupted by the One Ring for nearly 500 years, he is split between his malicious 'Gollum' persona and his gentler 'Sméagol' side.",
        "The Hobbit (1937) / The Lord of the Rings (1954), J.R.R. Tolkien",
        "Gollum, the wretched creature, curls his spine like a bicycle chain as he hunches forward, his body coiling into a figure-skater's spin. He whispers 'My precious...' Smell the wet cave and rotting fish. Taste the raw fish oil on his lips. Feel the Ring's cold weight in his palm. Hear his own split voice cry 'GOLLUM! GOLLUM!' as the cave echoes back."),
    PAOEntry("39", "The Grinch", "Stealing", "a Christmas tree", "Fictional", "Holiday Antagonist (Dr. Seuss)",
        "The Grinch is the green, grouchy protagonist-antagonist of Dr. Seuss's 1957 book How the Grinch Stole Christmas! A bitter creature who lives in a cave on Mount Crumpit, he tries to ruin Whoville's Christmas by stealing all their presents.",
        "How the Grinch Stole Christmas! (1957), Dr. Seuss",
        "The Grinch, the green grouch, snatches a Christmas tree with his wavy scarf-shaped arms as it spins like a top. Smell the pine needles and his own sour breath. Taste the bitterness of his heart. Feel the tree needles prick his fingers. Hear the Whos singing below as the tree spins. He laughs 'You're a mean one, Mr. Grinch!' as his heart grows three sizes."),
    PAOEntry("40", "Cinderella", "Waltzing", "a glass slipper", "Fictional", "Fairy Tale Princess",
        "Cinderella is the protagonist of a classic fairy tale with versions dating back to ancient Greece and China, most famously recorded by Charles Perrault (1697). A kind girl forced into servitude by her stepmother, she attends a royal ball with fairy godmother magic.",
        "Cendrillon (1697), Charles Perrault / Disney's Cinderella (1950)",
        "Cinderella, the servant girl, spins in a straight-backed chair as the car tire rolls beneath her — she's on a magical carriage wheel. Smell the pumpkin and glass. Taste the sugar from the ball on her tongue. Feel the glass slipper on her foot. Hear the clock strike midnight. She yells 'I HAVE TO GO!' as the slipper slips off."),
    PAOEntry("41", "The Flash", "Sprinting", "a lightning bolt", "Fictional", "DC Superhero (Speedster)",
        "The Flash is the alter ego of forensic scientist Barry Allen, created by Robert Kanigher and Carmine Infantino in 1956. Struck by lightning and doused in chemicals, he gains super-speed and the ability to vibrate through matter and time-travel via the Speed Force.",
        "Showcase #4 (1956), DC Comics",
        "The Flash, the fastest man alive, sprints with a kite frame trailing behind him, an arrow shaft in his hand. He runs so fast he time-travels. Smell the ozone and friction. Taste the metallic speed on his tongue. Feel the wind tearing at his suit. Hear him yell 'I'M THE FASTEST MAN ALIVE!' as his shoes catch fire. He stops and says 'Oops.'"),
    PAOEntry("42", "Frodo Baggins", "Trekking", "a sack of potatoes", "Fictional", "Hobbit Ring-Bearer (LOTR)",
        "Frodo Baggins is the central protagonist of The Lord of the Rings, played by Elijah Wood. A hobbit from the Shire, he volunteers to carry the One Ring to Mordor to destroy it in the fires of Mount Doom.",
        "The Fellowship of the Ring (1954), J.R.R. Tolkien",
        "Frodo Baggins, the hobbit Ring-bearer, hikes up a flag-shaped mountain path, his leg buckling into a curved 2-shape as he drags a sack of potatoes behind him. Every time he slips, a potato rolls back down the flag-shaped path. Smell the cold volcanic ash. Taste dust on his cracked lips. Hear Sam's voice call 'MR. FRODO!' behind him. Feel the potatoes drag him down. By the end, he's crying — hot tears — chasing his own potatoes downhill, his curved leg shaking, the flag at the peak mocking him."),
    PAOEntry("43", "The Terminator", "Terminating", "a shotgun", "Fictional", "Cyborg Assassin (Terminator)",
        "The Terminator is a cybernetic assassin played by Arnold Schwarzenegger, first appearing in James Cameron's 1984 film. A T-800 model sent back in time to kill Sarah Connor, it is famous for the line 'I'll be back.'",
        "The Terminator (1984), James Cameron",
        "The Terminator, the cyborg assassin, grips a shotgun with a taco-shell-shaped barrel as he walks past a goalpost. He yells 'I'LL BE BACK!' and shoots the jukebox in a biker bar. Smell the gunpowder and leather. Taste the synthetic saliva in his mouth. Feel the shotgun's cold steel in his metal hand. Hear the bikers scatter, and the jukebox crackle and die."),
    PAOEntry("44", "Jack Frost", "Nibbling", "a snowflake", "Fictional", "Winter Folklore Figure",
        "Jack Frost is the personification of winter in English folklore, said to leave frosty patterns on windows and nip at the noses of those out in the cold. He has appeared in many stories, most famously in the 2012 DreamWorks film Rise of the Guardians.",
        "English folklore / Rise of the Guardians (2012), DreamWorks",
        "Jack Frost, the winter spirit, skis down a mountain with two sailboat masts strapped to his feet as ice sprays. He blows icy breath. Smell the fresh snow and pine. Taste the frost on his lips. Feel the cold wind on his face. Hear him laugh 'The cold never bothered me anyway!' as the lake freezes solid behind him."),
    PAOEntry("45", "Lara Croft", "Discovering", "a relic", "Fictional", "Tomb Raider (Video Game & Film)",
        "Lara Croft is the protagonist of the Tomb Raider franchise, created by Toby Gard in 1996. An English archaeologist and adventurer, she explores ancient ruins, tombs, and temples to uncover relics — often fighting mercenaries and supernatural forces.",
        "Tomb Raider (1996), Core Design / Eidos Interactive",
        "Lara Croft, the tomb raider, swings a hockey stick at a sickle-shaped relic in a hidden tomb. She whacks it, and the relic flies into her hand. Smell the dust and ancient stone. Taste the dry air on her tongue. Feel the relic's cold gold in her palm. Hear her yell 'AHA!' as the ceiling starts to collapse. She runs out as the tomb crumbles."),
    PAOEntry("46", "Mary Poppins", "Umbrella-dancing", "a carpet bag", "Fictional", "Magical Nanny (Children's Literature)",
        "Mary Poppins is the magical nanny created by P.L. Travers in 1934, famously portrayed by Julie Andrews in the 1964 Disney film. She arrives at the Banks family home carried by an umbrella, carries a bottomless carpet bag, and brings joy and wonder.",
        "Mary Poppins (1934), P.L. Travers / Disney's Mary Poppins (1964)",
        "Mary Poppins, the magical nanny, opens her road-sign-shaped umbrella and lifts a cherry-stem-shaped carpet bag. She floats into the sky, singing. Smell the soot and chimney smoke. Taste the sugar on her tongue. Feel the umbrella's handle in her hand. Hear her sing 'A spoonful of sugar!' as the bag pulls out a lamp. She lands gently, and the bag closes."),
    PAOEntry("47", "Baba Yaga", "Stirring", "a mortar", "Fictional", "Slavic Folklore Witch",
        "Baba Yaga is a fearsome witch from Slavic folklore who lives in a hut standing on chicken legs deep in the forest. She flies around in a giant mortar, using the pestle as a rudder and sweeping away her tracks with a broom.",
        "Slavic folklore (Eastern Europe, 18th century+)",
        "Baba Yaga, the Slavic witch, stirs her cauldron with a water-tower-leg-shaped pestle as a straight fishhook hangs above. She cackles 'Fee fi fo fum!' and the potion explodes into green smoke. Smell the sulfur and swamp. Taste the bitter herbs on her tongue. Feel the pestle's rough wood. Hear the cauldron bubble and hiss. She coughs, and the cauldron falls over."),
    PAOEntry("48", "The Mad Hatter", "Sipping", "a teacup", "Fictional", "Wonderland Character (Alice in Wonderland)",
        "The Mad Hatter is a character from Lewis Carroll's Alice's Adventures in Wonderland (1865). He hosts an endless tea party with the March Hare and the Dormouse, constantly asking riddles like 'Why is a raven like a writing desk?'",
        "Alice's Adventures in Wonderland (1865), Lewis Carroll",
        "The Mad Hatter, the Wonderland character, balances on a cross stance while ringing jester bells and pouring tea into a giant cup. He yells 'CHANGE PLACES!' as the cup shatters. Smell the chamomile and biscuits. Taste the sugar on his lips. Feel the teapot's warmth in his hand. Hear the March Hare and Dormouse singing. The tea floods the table, and everyone laughs."),
    PAOEntry("49", "The Tin Man", "Axing", "a tree", "Fictional", "Wizard of Oz Character",
        "The Tin Woodman (Tin Man) is a character in L. Frank Baum's The Wonderful Wizard of Oz (1900). A man made entirely of tin after a cursed axe cut away his body, he joins Dorothy on her journey to the Emerald City to ask the Wizard for a heart.",
        "The Wonderful Wizard of Oz (1900), L. Frank Baum",
        "The Tin Man, the metal woodsman, swings a compass-needle axe as his bent-fishhook arm creaks. He chops a tree down with a CLANG. Smell the oil and rust. Taste the tin in his mouth. Feel the axe handle in his hands. Hear him yell 'IF I ONLY HAD A HEART!' as oil leaks from his joints. He cries, and Dorothy pats his back."),
    PAOEntry("50", "King Arthur", "Pulling", "Excalibur from stone", "Fictional", "Legendary British King (Arthurian Legend)",
        "King Arthur is the legendary ruler of Camelot in British folklore, first appearing in the 12th-century writings of Geoffrey of Monmouth. He proved his right to rule by pulling the sword Excalibur from a stone as a boy.",
        "Historia Regum Britanniae (1136), Geoffrey of Monmouth",
        "King Arthur pulls Excalibur from a gold medal-shaped stone, his curled fist gripping the hilt. The sword glows as the stone shatters. Smell the wet earth and ancient magic. Taste the copper of the sword on his tongue. Feel the cold pommel in his palm. Hear the blade sing as it comes free. He raises it high, and light floods the courtyard."),
    PAOEntry("51", "Medusa", "Petrifying", "a mirror", "Fictional", "Greek Mythological Gorgon",
        "Medusa is a Gorgon from Greek mythology, one of three sisters with snakes for hair. Anyone who looks directly at her face turns to stone. She was slain by the hero Perseus, who used a mirrored shield to avoid her gaze and then beheaded her.",
        "Greek Mythology (Homer's Iliad, 8th century BC onward)",
        "Medusa, the Gorgon, flicks her forked tongue like a question-mark tail as she holds a thermometer-shaped mirror. She stares at her own reflection. Smell the swamp and stone. Taste the venom in her mouth. Feel the snakes writhing on her head. Hear them hiss as the mirror cracks. She screams, and the mirror shatters — but her reflection is already stone."),
    PAOEntry("52", "Anubis", "Weighing", "a heart", "Fictional", "Egyptian God of the Dead",
        "Anubis is the jackal-headed Egyptian god of mummification and the afterlife. He oversees the weighing of the heart ceremony, in which a deceased person's heart is weighed against the feather of Ma'at (truth and justice).",
        "Ancient Egyptian Mythology (c. 3100 BC onward)",
        "Anubis, the jackal-headed god, holds a genie-lamp-shaped scale and a leopard-tail feather. He weighs a heart against the feather. Smell the incense and dust. Taste the tomb air on his tongue. Feel the scale's cold balance in his hand. Hear the feather settle, and the scales tip. The heart is pure — or is it? He growls, and the chamber darkens."),
    PAOEntry("53", "Icarus", "Melting", "wax wings", "Fictional", "Greek Mythological Figure",
        "Icarus is a figure from Greek mythology, the son of the master craftsman Daedalus. He and his father escaped the labyrinth of Crete using wings made of feathers and wax. Despite his father's warnings, Icarus flew too close to the sun, the wax melted, and he fell into the sea.",
        "Greek Mythology (Ovid's Metamorphoses, 8 AD)",
        "Icarus, the boy of wax wings, beats his fig-roll-shaped wings as he climbs a staircase of clouds toward the sun. Smell the melting wax and burning feathers. Taste the salt of his own sweat. Feel the sun scorch his back. Hear his father scream 'ICARUS!' as the wax drips. He falls — feathers scattering — into the sea."),
    PAOEntry("54", "Godzilla", "Crushing", "Tokyo Tower", "Fictional", "Kaiju (Japanese Monster Films)",
        "Godzilla (Gojira) is a giant reptilian monster who first appeared in Ishirō Honda's 1954 film, inspired by the atomic bombings of Japan. Awakened by nuclear testing, he rampages through Tokyo and other cities, crushing buildings underfoot.",
        "Gojira (1954), Toho Studios",
        "Godzilla, the kaiju, stomps down a diving-board-shaped tail onto a toll-booth-shaped building. The building crushes under his foot. Smell the fire and smoke. Taste the ash on his tongue. Feel the ground shake. Hear him roar as Tokyo Tower bends. Cars fly through the air like toys."),
    PAOEntry("55", "The Invisible Man", "Looming", "a raincoat", "Fictional", "Sci-Fi Horror (H.G. Wells)",
        "The Invisible Man is the protagonist of H.G. Wells's 1897 sci-fi novel. A scientist named Griffin discovers a way to make himself invisible, but cannot reverse the process. Slowly driven insane, he wraps himself in bandages and a raincoat to hide his invisibility.",
        "The Invisible Man (1897), H.G. Wells",
        "The Invisible Man, wrapped in a raincoat, leaves two hooked-hand-shaped gloves floating in midair. He looms over you, unseen. Smell the damp wool and rain. Taste the metallic tang of fear. Feel the cold air shift as he moves. Hear his breath — but see nothing. He laughs from somewhere, and the gloves twitch."),
    PAOEntry("56", "Quetzalcoatl", "Coiling", "a pyramid", "Fictional", "Aztec Feathered Serpent God",
        "Quetzalcoatl is the Aztec god of wind, wisdom, and the morning star, depicted as a feathered serpent. Worshipped across Mesoamerica for over 2,000 years, he was associated with the planet Venus, learning, and the creation of humanity.",
        "Aztec & Mesoamerican Mythology (c. 100 BC onward)",
        "Quetzalcoatl, the feathered serpent, uncoils from a banana-peel-shaped twist as he spirals up a kettle-spout-shaped pyramid. Smell the jungle and rain. Taste the sweet fruit on his tongue. Feel the feathers brush your cheek. Hear his rattle-like hiss. He coils at the top and spreads his wings — light glinting off his scales."),
    PAOEntry("57", "Mothman", "Glowing", "red eyes", "Fictional", "Cryptid (American Urban Legend)",
        "Mothman is a cryptid reportedly seen in Point Pleasant, West Virginia, between 1966 and 1967. Described as a large, winged humanoid with glowing red eyes, he was blamed for numerous sightings and the collapse of the Silver Bridge on December 15, 1967.",
        "Point Pleasant Sightings (1966–1967), West Virginia",
        "Mothman, the cryptid, hovers with ice-cream-scoop-shaped wings while a crowbar-shaped antenna sways. His red eyes glow. Smell the ozone and river water. Taste the cold on your tongue. Feel the dread in your stomach. Hear the flap of his wings. He points at the bridge — the Silver Bridge — and vanishes."),
    PAOEntry("58", "Bigfoot", "Trampling", "a campsite", "Fictional", "Cryptid (North American Folklore)",
        "Bigfoot (also Sasquatch) is a legendary ape-like creature said to inhabit the forests of North America. Described as a large, hairy, bipedal humanoid, he is most famous from the 1967 Patterson–Gimlin film. Despite thousands of sightings, no physical evidence has ever been confirmed.",
        "North American Folklore / Patterson–Gimlin Film (1967)",
        "Bigfoot, the cryptid, turns a lock-latch-shaped stick as he stomps past stacked-tire-shaped rocks. His footprints sink deep. Smell the pine and fur. Taste the moss on his tongue. Feel the earth shake. Hear the twig snap under his foot. He stops and looks at you — then vanishes into the trees."),
    PAOEntry("59", "La Llorona", "Weeping", "a river", "Fictional", "Latin American Folklore Ghost",
        "La Llorona (The Weeping Woman) is a ghost from Latin American folklore, said to have drowned her children in a river to be with a man who rejected her. Condemned to wander the earth, she weeps endlessly by rivers and waterways.",
        "Latin American Folklore (Mexico, 16th century onward)",
        "La Llorona, the Weeping Woman, clicks her tongue like a hooked lasso as she stares into a river of tears. She wails. Smell the river water and wet earth. Taste the salt of her own tears. Feel the grief in her chest. Hear her cry '¡Ay, mis hijos!' as the river swells."),
    PAOEntry("60", "Dracula", "Sucking", "a blood orange", "Fictional", "Vampire (Gothic Horror)",
        "Count Dracula is the vampire antagonist of Bram Stoker's 1897 Gothic horror novel. A centuries-old Transylvanian nobleman, he drinks the blood of the living to survive and can transform into a bat, wolf, or mist.",
        "Dracula (1897), Bram Stoker",
        "Dracula, the vampire, curls his shepherd's-crook-shaped fangs into a glass-marble-shaped blood orange. He sucks it dry. Smell the iron and old blood. Taste the copper on his lips. Feel the orange burst in his hand. Hear him hiss as the juice drips down his chin. He wipes his mouth and vanishes into the night."),
    PAOEntry("61", "Kali", "Brandishing", "a sword", "Fictional", "Hindu Goddess of Destruction",
        "Kali is the Hindu goddess of time, destruction, and change. Often depicted with dark blue skin, multiple arms, and a necklace of severed heads, she wields a sword and is the fierce consort of Lord Shiva.",
        "Hindu Mythology (Devi Mahatmya, 6th century AD)",
        "Kali, the Hindu goddess, files her nails with a nail-file-shaped blade as a rope-knot-shaped necklace jangles. She holds a severed head. Smell the blood and incense. Taste the copper on her tongue. Feel the blade bite her nail. Hear her laugh as the head rolls away. Her multiple arms wave in the smoky light."),
    PAOEntry("62", "Fenrir", "Biting", "a chain", "Fictional", "Norse Mythological Wolf",
        "Fenrir is a monstrous wolf from Norse mythology, the son of Loki and the giantess Angrboða. Prophesied to devour Odin during Ragnarök, he was bound by the gods using a magical ribbon called Gleipnir.",
        "Norse Mythology (Prose Edda, 13th century)",
        "Fenrir, the giant wolf, bares his fox-ear-shaped fangs as he bites a chain wrapped around a round-belly-shaped boulder. He snarls. Smell the wolf musk and iron. Taste the metal on his tongue. Feel the chain bite into his jaw. Hear him howl as the chain snaps. The gods tremble in Asgard."),
    PAOEntry("63", "Banshee", "Shrieking", "a megaphone", "Fictional", "Irish Mythological Spirit",
        "A Banshee (from Irish 'bean sídhe,' meaning 'woman of the fairy mound') is a female spirit in Irish and Scottish folklore. Her piercing wail is said to foretell the death of a family member.",
        "Irish & Scottish Folklore (8th century onward)",
        "The Banshee, the Irish spirit, shrieks with a bell-curve-shaped mouth into a bent-bow-shaped megaphone. Her wail pierces the night. Smell the graveyard and fog. Taste the salt of her tears. Feel the chill in the air. Hear her cry echo across the moors. A family somewhere hears her — and knows someone will die."),
    PAOEntry("64", "Sun Wukong", "Transforming", "a staff", "Fictional", "Chinese Mythological Monkey King",
        "Sun Wukong, the Monkey King, is the protagonist of the 16th-century Chinese novel Journey to the West. Born from a stone egg, he has the power of 72 transformations and wields the Ruyi Jingu Bang — a magical staff that can grow or shrink at will.",
        "Journey to the West (16th century), Wu Cheng'en",
        "Sun Wukong, the Monkey King, transforms his kettle-spout-shaped staff into an arrowhead-shaped spear as he rides a cloud. He laughs. Smell the peaches and rain. Taste the sweet fruit on his tongue. Feel the cloud under his feet. Hear him shout 'I AM THE GREAT SAGE!' as he swings the spear."),
    PAOEntry("65", "Cthulhu", "Awakening", "an ancient tome", "Fictional", "Cosmic Horror (H.P. Lovecraft)",
        "Cthulhu is a cosmic entity created by H.P. Lovecraft in 1928. Described as a giant, tentacled, humanoid dragon sleeping in the sunken city of R'lyeh, he telepathically influences dreamers. When the stars align, he will awaken and destroy humanity.",
        "The Call of Cthulhu (1928), H.P. Lovecraft",
        "Cthulhu, the cosmic horror, rises from the sea with a teacup-handle-shaped tentacle curling around a door-knocker-shaped tome. His presence warps reality. Smell the saltwater and rot. Taste the madness on your tongue. Feel your mind crack. Hear him groan — a sound that shatters glass. You scream, but no sound comes out."),
    PAOEntry("66", "Kitsune", "Shapeshifting", "nine tails", "Fictional", "Japanese Fox Spirit",
        "A Kitsune is a fox spirit from Japanese folklore. They are intelligent, magical creatures who can shapeshift into beautiful women, possess people, and grow up to nine tails as they age — each tail representing centuries of wisdom.",
        "Japanese Folklore (10th century onward)",
        "Kitsune, the fox spirit, weaves two rolled-newspaper-shaped tails as she spins a jump-rope-loop-shaped ring of fire. She transforms. Smell the incense and fur. Taste the smoke on your tongue. Feel the heat of the flames. Hear her laugh as she becomes a beautiful woman. The fire ring closes and she bows."),
    PAOEntry("67", "Wendigo", "Shredding", "a frozen forest", "Fictional", "Algonquian Mythological Spirit",
        "The Wendigo is a malevolent, cannibalistic spirit from the folklore of the Algonquian-speaking peoples of the Great Lakes region. It is associated with winter, starvation, and the taboo against cannibalism. Depicted as a gaunt, skeletal creature with a heart of ice.",
        "Algonquian Folklore (Great Lakes region)",
        "The Wendigo, the cannibal spirit, drips safety-pin-curl-shaped icicles as it climbs a slanted-roof-shaped tree. It shrieks. Smell the frozen meat and pine. Taste the frost on your tongue. Feel the cold burn your skin. Hear the branches snap under its weight. It spots you — and grins."),
    PAOEntry("68", "Valkyrie", "Guiding", "a fallen warrior", "Fictional", "Norse Mythological Warrior Maiden",
        "Valkyries are warrior maidens in Norse mythology who serve Odin. Riding winged horses and bearing spears, they choose which warriors die in battle and which live. The fallen are taken to Valhalla, where they become Einherjar.",
        "Norse Mythology (Poetic Edda, 13th century)",
        "The Valkyrie, the warrior maiden, rides a whale-spout-shaped cloud as two cherry-shaped gems dangle from her spear. She descends. Smell the storm and iron. Taste the rain on your lips. Feel the wind tear at your clothes. Hear her battle cry as she scoops up a fallen warrior and carries him to Valhalla."),
    PAOEntry("69", "Cerberus", "Guarding", "a gate", "Fictional", "Greek Mythological Three-Headed Dog",
        "Cerberus is the three-headed dog who guards the gates of the Greek Underworld, preventing the dead from leaving and the living from entering. He is the offspring of the monsters Typhon and Echidna, and was captured by Heracles as his 12th labor.",
        "Greek Mythology (Hesiod's Theogony, 8th century BC)",
        "Cerberus, the three-headed dog, curls his ear-tip-shaped tails around a padlock-curve-shaped gate. He growls from three mouths. Smell the sulfur and blood. Taste the fear in your throat. Feel the ground shake under his paws. Hear all three heads bark at once — and the gates of the underworld slam shut."),
    PAOEntry("70", "Frida Kahlo", "Painting", "a self-portrait", "Real Person", "Mexican Painter",
        "Frida Kahlo (1907–1954) was a Mexican painter known for her many self-portraits exploring identity, pain, and Mexican folk culture. After a near-fatal bus accident at 18, she painted from her bed, often using a mirror above her.",
        "Real life / Mexican Modernism (1920s–1950s)",
        "Frida Kahlo, the Mexican painter, balances a stack-of-pancakes-shaped palette as she leans on a walking-cane-shaped brush. She paints her own face. Smell the oil paint and turpentine. Taste the bitterness of her lipstick. Feel the brush in her hand. Hear the brushstroke on canvas as she adds another flower to her hair."),
    PAOEntry("71", "Nikola Tesla", "Sparking", "a Tesla coil", "Real Person", "Serbian-American Inventor",
        "Nikola Tesla (1856–1943) was a Serbian-American inventor and electrical engineer. He pioneered the modern alternating current (AC) electricity system, developed the Tesla coil, and made breakthroughs in wireless power and radio.",
        "Real life / AC electricity (1880s–1940s)",
        "Nikola Tesla, the inventor, swings a scythe-blade-shaped coil as he leans on a walking-stick-shaped metal pole. Electricity arcs from his hands. Smell the ozone and burnt copper. Taste the metallic charge on his tongue. Feel the static lifting his hair. Hear him yell 'THE POWER OF THE UNIVERSE!' as lightning cracks the sky."),
    PAOEntry("72", "Marie Curie", "Isolating", "a radioactive isotope", "Real Person", "Polish-French Physicist & Chemist",
        "Marie Curie (1867–1934) was a Polish-French physicist and chemist who discovered polonium and radium. She was the first woman to win a Nobel Prize, the first person to win two Nobel Prizes in different sciences. Her notebooks are still radioactive today.",
        "Real life / Nobel Prizes in Physics (1903) and Chemistry (1911)",
        "Marie Curie, the physicist, stands at a cliff-edge-shaped precipice, her elbow-crook-shaped arm holding a glowing vial of radium. It burns green. Smell the chemicals and radiation. Taste the bitter metal on her tongue. Feel the heat of the vial through her glove. Hear the Geiger counter click faster. She smiles. 'This will change the world.'"),
    PAOEntry("73", "H.P. Lovecraft", "Chanting", "a Necronomicon", "Real Person", "American Horror Writer",
        "H.P. Lovecraft (1890–1937) was an American writer of weird and cosmic horror fiction. He created the Cthulhu Mythos, a shared universe of ancient gods and forbidden knowledge, and invented the fictional grimoire 'Necronomicon.'",
        "Real life / The Call of Cthulhu (1928)",
        "H.P. Lovecraft, the horror writer, swings a golf-club-shaped pen as a rope-coil-shaped tendril crawls across his desk. He writes furiously. Smell the ink and old paper. Taste the madness on his tongue. Feel the cold tendril on his wrist. Hear him whisper 'Ph'nglui mglw'nafh Cthulhu R'lyeh wgah'nagl fhtagn' as the room darkens."),
    PAOEntry("74", "Joan of Arc", "Rallying", "a banner", "Real Person", "French Military Hero & Saint",
        "Joan of Arc (1412–1431) was a French peasant girl who, guided by visions of saints, led French troops to key victories during the Hundred Years' War. She was captured by the English, tried for heresy, and burned at the stake at 19.",
        "Real life / Siege of Orléans (1429)",
        "Joan of Arc, the French heroine, raises a hockey-stick-shaped banner as an oar-blade-shaped sword flashes in her other hand. She charges into battle. Smell the smoke and blood. Taste the iron of her own conviction. Feel the horse surge beneath her. Hear her cry 'FOR FRANCE!' as the enemy scatters."),
    PAOEntry("75", "Leonardo da Vinci", "Sketching", "the Mona Lisa", "Real Person", "Italian Renaissance Polymath",
        "Leonardo da Vinci (1452–1519) was an Italian polymath of the Renaissance — painter, sculptor, architect, scientist, and inventor. He painted the Mona Lisa and The Last Supper, and filled over 13,000 pages of notebooks with sketches.",
        "Real life / Italian Renaissance (1452–1519)",
        "Leonardo da Vinci, the Renaissance genius, sketches the Mona Lisa with a ski-pole-shaped brush while a carabiner-shaped palette hangs from his belt. Smell the oil paint and old parchment. Taste the bitterness of his own focus. Feel the brush's fine tip on the canvas. Hear the scratch of bristles as her smile appears. He steps back — 'Perfection.'"),
    PAOEntry("76", "Virginia Woolf", "Writing", "a diary", "Real Person", "English Modernist Author",
        "Virginia Woolf (1882–1941) was an English writer and a pioneer of the stream-of-consciousness narrative style. Her works include Mrs Dalloway, To the Lighthouse, and Orlando. She was a central figure of the Bloomsbury Group.",
        "Real life / Modernist Literature (1915–1941)",
        "Virginia Woolf, the modernist writer, writes in her diary with a shepherd's-staff-shaped pen while a key-fob-shaped pendant dangles. Smell the ink and old paper. Taste the salt of her tears on her lips. Feel the pen's cold metal. Hear the scratch of the nib on paper. She writes: 'I am rooted, but I flow.'"),
    PAOEntry("77", "Mozart", "Composing", "a piano", "Real Person", "Austrian Classical Composer",
        "Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart (1756–1791) was a prolific Austrian composer of the Classical era. A child prodigy who began composing at age 5, he wrote over 600 works — symphonies, operas, concertos, and chamber music — before dying at just 35.",
        "Real life / Classical Era (1756–1791)",
        "Mozart, the composer, sits at a piano with two crossed piano-leg-shaped pedals. His fingers fly across the keys. Smell the wood and rosin. Taste the sweetness of the music on his tongue. Feel the keys' ivory under his fingers. Hear the symphony rise as he plays. He smiles — a genius at work."),
    PAOEntry("78", "Amelia Earhart", "Navigating", "a biplane", "Real Person", "American Aviation Pioneer",
        "Amelia Earhart (1897–1937) was an American aviation pioneer and author. She was the first woman to fly solo across the Atlantic Ocean (1932) and the first person to fly solo from Hawaii to California. She disappeared over the Pacific in 1937.",
        "Real life / Transatlantic Flight (1932)",
        "Amelia Earhart, the aviator, grips the umbrella-shaft-shaped controls of her biplane while her goggle-lenses-shaped eyes scan the horizon. Smell the fuel and leather. Taste the cold wind on her lips. Feel the plane vibrate under her hands. Hear the engine roar. She yells 'I'M GOING AROUND THE WORLD!' as the ocean glitters below."),
    PAOEntry("79", "Bruce Lee", "Wielding", "a nunchuck", "Real Person", "Martial Artist & Actor",
        "Bruce Lee (1940–1973) was a Hong Kong-American martial artist, actor, and philosopher. He founded the martial art Jeet Kune Do and starred in iconic films like Enter the Dragon. He was famous for his lightning-fast speed and one-inch punch.",
        "Real life / Enter the Dragon (1973)",
        "Bruce Lee, the martial arts legend, wields a lightning-rod-shaped nunchuck with a yo-yo-string-shaped chain. He spins it faster than the eye can follow. Smell the sweat and sandalwood. Taste the focus in his mouth. Feel the nunchuck's wood in his hands. Hear the whoosh of each swing. He yells 'BE WATER, MY FRIEND!' as the chain snaps and the nunchuck flies."),
    PAOEntry("80", "Picasso", "Fragmenting", "a guitar", "Real Person", "Spanish Cubist Painter",
        "Pablo Picasso (1881–1973) was a Spanish painter, sculptor, and co-founder of the Cubist movement. His works include Les Demoiselles d'Avignon and Guernica. He painted over 20,000 works across his 78-year career.",
        "Real life / Cubism (1881–1973)",
        "Picasso, the Cubist painter, fragments a guitar into pieces shaped like two stacked mushrooms sitting on a fried-egg-shaped palette. He glues the pieces back sideways. Smell the paint and turpentine. Taste the bitterness of his own genius. Feel the shattered wood in his hands. Hear him laugh as the guitar screams 'HELP ME!' Picasso calls it art and ignores it."),
    PAOEntry("81", "Han Solo", "Shooting", "a Stormtrooper", "Fictional", "Smuggler (Star Wars)",
        "Han Solo is a smuggler and captain of the Millennium Falcon in the Star Wars saga, played by Harrison Ford. A cynical rogue with a heart of gold, he becomes a hero of the Rebel Alliance. His famous 'shoots first' scene with Greedo is one of cinema's most debated moments.",
        "Star Wars: Episode IV — A New Hope (1977)",
        "Han Solo, the smuggler, fires his billiard-cue-shaped blaster at a Stormtrooper, his wide-open eyes scanning the cantina. Smell the smoke and sweat. Taste the adrenaline on his tongue. Feel the blaster's grip in his hand. Hear the shot and the Stormtrooper fall. He yells 'I SHOOT FIRST!' and runs."),
    PAOEntry("82", "Obi-Wan Kenobi", "Vanishing", "a horse", "Fictional", "Jedi Master (Star Wars)",
        "Obi-Wan Kenobi is a legendary Jedi Master in the Star Wars saga, played by Alec Guinness and Ewan McGregor. He trained Anakin Skywalker and later his son Luke. In A New Hope, he allows Darth Vader to strike him down, becoming one with the Force.",
        "Star Wars: Episode IV — A New Hope (1977)",
        "Obi-Wan Kenobi, the Jedi Master, vanishes into the Force with a figure-skater-spin-shaped flourish as his guitar-neck-shaped lightsaber falls. He becomes one with the Force. Smell the scorched metal. Taste the calm on his tongue. Feel the Force ripple through the air. Hear him whisper 'If you strike me down, I shall become more powerful than you can possibly imagine.' He vanishes."),
    PAOEntry("83", "Zorro", "Slicing", "a 'Z' mark", "Fictional", "Masked Vigilante (Spanish California)",
        "Zorro (Spanish for 'fox') is a masked outlaw created by Johnston McCulley in 1919. The alter ego of nobleman Don Diego de la Vega, he defends the people of Spanish California against corrupt officials, carving a 'Z' with his rapier as his signature.",
        "The Curse of Capistrano (1919), Johnston McCulley",
        "Zorro, the masked vigilante, slices a clam-shell-ridge-shaped 'Z' mark into a wall with his sword as stacked-coin-shaped sparks fly. Smell the blood and dust. Taste the satisfaction on his tongue. Feel the sword's blade in his hand. Hear the 'Z' sing into the wall. He bows and disappears into the night."),
    PAOEntry("84", "Mulan", "Sword-fighting", "a dragon companion", "Fictional", "Chinese Legendary Warrior",
        "Hua Mulan is a legendary Chinese warrior from the Ballad of Mulan (5th century). She disguises herself as a man to take her aging father's place in the army, fighting for 12 years without her identity being discovered.",
        "Ballad of Mulan (5th century) / Disney's Mulan (1998)",
        "Mulan, the warrior, swings a jester-bells-shaped sword while a fence-corner-shaped shield deflects arrows. She rides into battle. Smell the smoke and blood. Taste the courage on her tongue. Feel the horse surge beneath her. Hear her battle cry as she charges. The enemy scatters."),
    PAOEntry("85", "Ragnar Lothbrok", "Raiding", "a longship", "Fictional", "Viking King & Legendary Hero",
        "Ragnar Lothbrok is a legendary Viking hero and king from Old Norse poetry and sagas. He is said to have raided England, France, and Scandinavia in the 9th century, and was famously killed by King Ælla of Northumbria by being thrown into a pit of snakes.",
        "Norse Sagas (13th century) / Vikings TV series (2013)",
        "Ragnar Lothbrok, the Viking king, holds a double-scoop-ice-cream-shaped axe while a lock-latch-shaped shield hangs from his arm. He charges into battle. Smell the blood and sea salt. Taste the iron of his own conviction. Feel the axe's weight in his palm. Hear his war cry as his longship hits the beach."),
    PAOEntry("86", "Blackbeard", "Pirating", "a treasure map", "Real Person", "English Pirate (Golden Age)",
        "Blackbeard, born Edward Teach (c. 1680–1718), was an English pirate who terrorized the West Indies and the American colonies. He famously lit slow-burning fuses in his beard to look like a demon from hell. He was killed by Lieutenant Robert Maynard.",
        "Real life / Golden Age of Piracy (1716–1718)",
        "Blackbeard, the pirate, holds a licorice-twist-shaped map while an infinity-loop-shaped compass spins in his hand. He laughs as his ship approaches. Smell the gunpowder and rum. Taste the salt on his lips. Feel the ship's deck under his boots. Hear his crew roar as the cannons fire."),
    PAOEntry("87", "Genghis Khan", "Conquering", "a horse", "Real Person", "Founder of the Mongol Empire",
        "Genghis Khan (c. 1162–1227) was the founder and first Great Khan of the Mongol Empire, the largest contiguous land empire in history. He united the nomadic tribes of Northeast Asia and conquered vast territories from China to Eastern Europe.",
        "Real life / Mongol Empire (1206–1227)",
        "Genghis Khan, the conqueror, swings a crowbar-shaped sword while bongo-drum-shaped war drums beat behind him. He rides into battle. Smell the dust and blood. Taste the iron in his mouth. Feel the horse's mane under his hand. Hear his war cry as he charges. The enemy scatters."),
    PAOEntry("88", "Achilles", "Battling", "a heel", "Fictional", "Greek Mythological Warrior",
        "Achilles is the greatest warrior of the Trojan War in Homer's Iliad. His mother Thetis dipped him in the River Styx as a baby to make him invulnerable, but held him by the heel — leaving that one spot mortal. He was killed by an arrow to the heel.",
        "Greek Mythology / Homer's Iliad (8th century BC)",
        "Achilles, the Greek warrior, clashes two round shields together — CLANG! CLANG! He roars. Smell the blood and sweat of Troy. Taste the iron of his own bitten lip. Feel the leather grip of each shield in his palms. Hear the shields clash again. He screams a war cry as a Trojan falls. His heel — his only weak spot — twitches behind him, forgotten."),
    PAOEntry("89", "Socrates", "Debating", "a hemlock cup", "Real Person", "Ancient Greek Philosopher",
        "Socrates (c. 470–399 BC) was a classical Greek philosopher credited as one of the founders of Western philosophy. He taught through questioning — the Socratic method — and was executed by drinking hemlock poison after being convicted of impiety.",
        "Real life / Classical Athens (469–399 BC)",
        "Socrates, the philosopher, leans on a bell-curve-tail-shaped cane while a wobbly-wheel-shaped chariot rolls past. He debates. Smell the olive oil and dust. Taste the bitter hemlock on his tongue. Feel the cane's worn wood in his hand. Hear his voice rise: 'The unexamined life is not worth living!' The chariot rolls on."),
    PAOEntry("90", "Nostradamus", "Predicting", "a crystal ball", "Real Person", "French Astrologer & Seer",
        "Nostradamus (1503–1566) was a French astrologer, physician, and reputed seer. He is best known for his book Les Prophéties (1555), a collection of 942 quatrains allegedly predicting future events. Many believe he foretold the French Revolution and the rise of Hitler.",
        "Real life / Les Prophéties (1555)",
        "Nostradamus, the astrologer, watches a crystal-ball-shaped orb float on a balloon-on-string-shaped tether. He sees the future. Smell the incense and old books. Taste the bitter herbs on his tongue. Feel the orb's cold glass in his hand. Hear the future whisper — 'The end is near!' — as the orb cracks."),
    PAOEntry("91", "Spartacus", "Gladiating", "a trident", "Real Person", "Thracian Gladiator & Rebel Leader",
        "Spartacus (c. 111–71 BC) was a Thracian gladiator who escaped from a Roman gladiator school and led a major slave uprising against the Roman Republic — the Third Servile War. His army of 70,000+ rebels defeated multiple Roman legions before being crushed.",
        "Real life / Third Servile War (73–71 BC)",
        "Spartacus, the gladiator, swings a snail-shell-shaped trident while a baseball-bat-shaped gladius flashes in his other hand. He roars into battle. Smell the sand and blood. Taste the iron on his tongue. Feel the trident's wooden shaft in his hand. Hear the crowd roar as he charges. His enemy falls."),
    PAOEntry("92", "Frankenstein's Monster", "Reanimating", "a corpse", "Fictional", "Gothic Horror (Mary Shelley)",
        "Frankenstein's Monster (often called simply 'Frankenstein') is the creature created by Victor Frankenstein in Mary Shelley's 1818 novel. Assembled from corpses and brought to life by electricity, he is intelligent but rejected by society for his appearance.",
        "Frankenstein; or, The Modern Prometheus (1818), Mary Shelley",
        "Frankenstein's Monster, the reanimated creature, holds a yo-yo-string-shaped chain while a vine-tendril-shaped surgical tool hangs from his hand. He lurches forward. Smell the formaldehyde and rot. Taste the bitter metal on his tongue. Feel the chain's cold links in his palm. Hear him groan as lightning strikes the lab — and he rises."),
    PAOEntry("93", "Jekyll & Hyde", "Transforming", "a potion", "Fictional", "Victorian Gothic Horror",
        "Dr. Henry Jekyll and Mr. Edward Hyde are two sides of the same character in Robert Louis Stevenson's 1886 novella. Dr. Jekyll, a respectable scientist, creates a potion that transforms him into the evil, violent Mr. Hyde.",
        "Strange Case of Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde (1886), R.L. Stevenson",
        "Jekyll and Hyde, the dual personality, transforms with a cat's-curled-tail-shaped grin while a curled-chest-shaped potion bubbles in his hand. He drinks it. Smell the chemicals and sweat. Taste the bitterness on his tongue. Feel the transformation tear through his body. Hear him scream as Hyde takes over."),
    PAOEntry("94", "Nosferatu", "Screeching", "a coffin lid", "Fictional", "Silent Film Vampire",
        "Count Orlok, known as Nosferatu, is the vampire in F.W. Murnau's 1922 silent German film — an unauthorized adaptation of Bram Stoker's Dracula. Played by Max Schreck, he is depicted as a rat-like, bald, pointy-eared creature with long claws.",
        "Nosferatu: A Symphony of Horror (1922), F.W. Murnau",
        "Nosferatu, the silent-film vampire, lifts a rolled-ribbon-shaped coffin lid while a ski-pole-shaped claw reaches for you. He screeches. Smell the rot and old blood. Taste the fear in your throat. Feel the cold draft from the coffin. Hear his scream as he rises from the tomb."),
    PAOEntry("95", "Rasputin", "Surviving", "poison", "Real Person", "Russian Mystic & Royal Advisor",
        "Grigori Rasputin (1869–1916) was a Russian mystic and self-proclaimed holy man who became a trusted advisor to Tsar Nicholas II and Tsarina Alexandra. He was notoriously difficult to kill — he survived cyanide poisoning, a gunshot, and a beating before finally drowning.",
        "Real life / Russian Empire (1869–1916)",
        "Rasputin, the mad monk, drinks poison from a bicycle-bell-shaped goblet while a fishhook-shaped cross hangs from his neck. He smiles. Smell the poison and incense. Taste the bitter cyanide on his tongue. Feel the poison burn his throat. Hear him laugh: 'I am still alive!' — and he drinks more."),
    PAOEntry("96", "Medea", "Enchanting", "a Golden Fleece", "Fictional", "Greek Mythological Sorceress",
        "Medea is a sorceress in Greek mythology, granddaughter of the sun god Helios. She helped Jason obtain the Golden Fleece and fled with him to Corinth. When Jason later abandoned her, she took horrific revenge — killing their children.",
        "Greek Mythology / Euripides' Medea (431 BC)",
        "Medea, the sorceress, sprinkles a lollipop-stick-curve-shaped wand over a shepherd's-crook-shaped Golden Fleece. It glows. Smell the magic and incense. Taste the honey-sweet spell on her tongue. Feel the wand's warmth in her hand. Hear her chant as the fleece turns gold."),
    PAOEntry("97", "Odysseus", "Sailing", "a Trojan horse", "Fictional", "Greek Hero (Homer's Odyssey)",
        "Odysseus is the cunning king of Ithaca and protagonist of Homer's Odyssey. He devised the Trojan Horse — a wooden horse hiding Greek soldiers — which ended the Trojan War. His 10-year journey home was filled with monsters, gods, and temptation.",
        "Greek Mythology / Homer's Odyssey (8th century BC)",
        "Odysseus, the Greek king, sails with a fishing-bobber-shaped compass while a cutlass-shaped sword hangs from his belt. He commands his ship. Smell the salt and sea. Taste the brine on his lips. Feel the ship's wheel in his hands. Hear his crew row as he yells 'ROW! ROW FOR YOUR LIVES!'"),
    PAOEntry("98", "Beowulf", "Slaying", "Grendel", "Fictional", "Old English Epic Hero",
        "Beowulf is the hero of the oldest surviving Old English epic poem (c. 700–1000 AD). He travels to Denmark to help King Hrothgar, whose hall Heorot is being terrorized by the monster Grendel. Beowulf kills Grendel with his bare hands by ripping off his arm.",
        "Beowulf (Old English Epic, c. 700–1000 AD)",
        "Beowulf, the hero, grips a magnifying-handle-shaped sword while a jar-lid-on-jar-shaped shield hangs from his arm. He charges at Grendel. Smell the blood and mead. Taste the iron on his tongue. Feel the sword's hilt in his hand. Hear him yell 'I AM BEOWULF!' as he rips Grendel's arm off."),
    PAOEntry("99", "Persephone", "Descending", "a pomegranate", "Fictional", "Greek Goddess of Spring & Queen of Underworld",
        "Persephone is the daughter of Zeus and Demeter in Greek mythology. She was abducted by Hades, god of the Underworld, but before returning to the surface she ate six pomegranate seeds — binding her to spend six months of each year in the underworld. This myth explains the seasons.",
        "Greek Mythology / Homeric Hymn to Demeter (7th century BC)",
        "The first 9 is a round pomegranate. The second 9 is another round pomegranate. Persephone clutches both as she descends.")
)

fun getPAO(number: String): PAOEntry? = PAO_DATA.find { it.number == number }
